- **2026-04-29 (Tier 3 post-advisor refinements — Esc dual-fire + Enter fallback + paste-while-focused)** — Advisor reviewed Tier 3 immediately after landing and flagged three issues, all addressed in `src/ui/app.tsx` without re-running the architectural choice: (1) **CRITICAL — Esc dual-fire.** Three separate Esc listeners (`renderer._internalKeyInput.onInternal("keypress")` line 2338, `renderer.prependInputHandler` line 2353, `renderer.stdin.on("data")` line 2367) each unconditionally call `interruptActiveRun`, which wipes the queue + aborts the run. The `handleKey` `key.preventDefault/stopPropagation` only affects the React handler chain — it does NOT propagate to raw-stdin listeners. So Esc-while-focused was clearing focus AND wiping the queue + aborting the run. Fixed by mirroring `queueFocusId` to a `queueFocusIdRef` via a 1-line `useEffect`, then gating each listener: if focus is held, `setQueueFocusId(null)` + early-return (or `return true` for `prependInputHandler` to mark consumed). Listener-effect deps stay stable (`[interruptActiveRun, renderer]`) — no re-subscribe on focus change. (2) **MINOR — stale focusId on Enter.** If the focused id has been shifted out by `finishTurnProcessing` between focus and Enter, `dispatchQueueAt(staleId)` returns false silently. Fixed by changing `handleSubmit` Enter-on-empty to `if (queueFocusId !== null) { if (!dispatchQueueAt(queueFocusId)) dispatchQueueHead(); } else dispatchQueueHead();` so a stale id falls through to head dispatch. (3) **MINOR — paste-while-focused.** `handlePaste` didn't drop focus, inconsistent with the typing-exits-focus rule in `handleKey`. Fixed by reading `queueFocusIdRef.current` near the top of `handlePaste` and clearing focus if held. **Lessons:** (a) **`React.preventDefault()` doesn't stop raw-stdin listeners** — when designing "Esc means X here, Y elsewhere," audit every input surface (React handler + raw-stdin + prepend-input-handler + internal-keypress), not just the React one. The four surfaces are independent event paths. (b) **Refs are the right idiom for "state read by long-lived raw-event listeners."** Mirroring focus state via `useEffect(() => { ref.current = state; }, [state])` lets the listeners read current focus without re-subscribing on every change. Same shape as `isProcessingRef`, `shellModeRef`, `showApiKeyModalRef` elsewhere in this file — canonical pattern when raw event handlers need to reflect React state.

